okay so this is just going to be a quick  anecdote on the question of how much  sleep does a physics major get in terms  of having to stay up late for  assignments and study and all kinds of  things because that's a question I get  asked reasonably often so my personal  take is that it's possible to go through  a physics degree without doing  all-nighters because I have met some  people who say they didn't do any I did  a few all-nighters and it's mostly  because of like what my personality was  which was that I would do it at the last  minute and it just worked for me at the  last minute because everything was in my  brain at the same time and the only time  I really did all-nighters was lab  reports and handing in like a thesis so  like Mona's thesis I just stayed here  for like 48 hours typing and like  probably the most tired or stressful  time was during a course we did where we  had to hand in a lab report every week  and so this is at least like maybe 10  pages of what you've done in an  experiment every week and for that  course I probably did an all-nighter or  at least a really late night every week  but otherwise like I would probably go  to bed quite early and get quite a lot  of sleep so you can definitely do it if  you if you manage your time yes that  definitely depends on your personality  and how you structure your work if you  want to you can just do all of your work  in the morning you can plan out how  you're going to study but I don't know  many people at all who ever did that if  you do that you can get all your work  done before 5 p.m. and just treat it  like a nine-to-five job someone like me  who needs sleep to function or they  don't function at all I would quite  often I could study up to 9:10 p.m.  during exam time for serious assignments  or for exams that were really close  together I might stay past midnight to  like 2:00 a.m. but then I could just go  to sleep and wake up at 9:00 and get  seven hours of sleep and it'd be fine so  like exams were a weird time I think  because like  common-sense advices to get lots of  sleep before an exam and make sure  you're well-rested and all this but  something about the nature of when you  have like many consecutive exam days  that's not always so possible when  you're still trying to like I don't know  in crayon information before the exam  which it's not also not an ideal  strategy but haven't quite a lot and I  would probably get like five hours sleep  before an exam was kind of normal for me  because I would stay up till midnight  studying with other people at uni go to  bed for five hours and then study again  from like 5:00 a.m. until the exam at  9:00 or something like that I remember  that schedule because that's what I used  to do but we had a friend or a couple of  friends that would just sleep overnight  or have a nap in the physics building  and then at breakfast at uni and walk to  the exam they'd had only had like 15  minutes sleep all night  that sounds horrendous but it happened  and they it worked and they got a good  grades they could do that  yeah just sleep for 15 minutes then walk  into an exam and get an A+ also crash  afterwards but their mind was like so  full of differential equations at the  time that they could it can work so  where is my sort of studying technique  was to wake up around 9:00 a.m. go into  uni and then just stay there until you  want to go to sleep you end up basically  just living physics for about two weeks  oh yeah exam kind of horrific but you do  at least get to sleep so it's yeah and  and when you finish your last exam it's  always like a release because you've  been in that physics mindset just almost  24/7 yeah yeah yeah so I think there's a  few different takes on this but I think  like what James said you can get through  it if you plan at your time work 9:00 to  5:00 like a normal job but there are  definitely times I've experienced and  most people I know in physics exam time  especially studying late 